      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Toronto Maple Leafs' season didn't end with a bang - it whimpered out over seven consecutive losses, a fitting coda to a campaign that promised everything and delivered nothing. A 32-36-14 record, eighth place in the Atlantic, 31 games behind the division leader: these are the numbers of a franchise in freefall, not a Original Six powerhouse. <strong>The Maple Leafs landed the first overall pick in the 2026 draft lottery</strong>, a silver lining that feels more like consolation than salvation for a fanbase that's watched this core disappoint for years. The question now isn't who they'll draft - it's whether they have the courage to blow it up entirely. Coaching changes loom. The front office is in flux. Free agency looms as a crossroads moment: do you patch the holes or start over? <strong>The real work begins in the boardroom, where management must decide whether this core can be salvaged or whether a genuine rebuild is the only honest path forward</strong>. Toronto's patience has worn thin. The market demands answers, not excuses.",
        "updated": "Updated May 8, 2026"
      },
