    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "16-21",
      "detail": "4th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays are drowning. Four straight losses - three of them to Tampa Bay, a team that shouldn't be beating anybody - have left Toronto at 16-21, nine and a half games behind in the AL East and staring at an 11th playoff seed that grows more theoretical by the day. This isn't a slump anymore. This is a crisis wearing a uniform.\n\n<strong>The Rays swept them out of the Dome last week, winning the finale 3-0 on May 6th</strong>, and there's no sugar to coat it: a team with championship aspirations doesn't get blanked at home by Tampa Bay. The pitching has been competent enough. The problem is the lineup has gone mute. Runs aren't coming. Momentum isn't coming. Nothing is coming except the calendar turning toward irrelevance.\n\nThe question now isn't whether Toronto can make a run - it's whether they can stop the bleeding before the season becomes a funeral march. <strong>They host the Angels tonight at 6:07 PM ET</strong>, a team they should beat, but \"should\" is a word that's lost all meaning in this stretch. This game matters. The next three matter more. Toronto's window is slamming shut.",
        "updated": "Updated May 8, 2026"
      },
